{
  "$comment": "Machine-readable parameter contract for the projections named in api.md. The prose there stays the narrative; this file is what the implementations check against. Both languages carry the same table as a constant in their core (the core reads no files by design) and a test in each language asserts the constant equals this file — so the two cannot drift apart.",
  "formatVersion": "0.6",
  "types": {
    "integer": "a whole number. JSON knows no int/float split, so 2026 and 2026.0 are the same value and both are accepted; 2026.4 is a caller mistake, not a value to round into shape. Bounded to 2^53-1 so that both language runtimes represent it exactly.",
    "string": "a non-empty string",
    "date": "a zoneless calendar date, YYYY-MM-DD",
    "boolean": "true or false",
    "object": "a JSON object. Its inner shape is the operation's business — this contract answers what a key MAY be, not what a voucher looks like",
    "array": "a JSON list. Its elements are the operation's business, for the same reason",
    "money": "an object carrying a decimal `amount` as a STRING, and usually a `currency`. Declared apart from `object` because the mistake it catches is a real one: an amount passed as a JSON number was silently ignored by every operation that read it with an is-object check, and the operation carried on with its default"
  },
  "rules": {
    "unknownParameter": "A parameter that is not declared here is rejected with E_INPUT_INVALID. Silently ignoring it turns a misspelled `fiscalYear` on vatReturn into a plausible annual figure where a quarter was asked for.",
    "absentOptional": "An undeclared-as-required parameter that is absent keeps its documented default. Absent is not the same as wrong.",
    "presentButInvalid": "A declared parameter present with a value of the wrong type is rejected with E_INPUT_INVALID — never coerced, never defaulted.",
    "acceptedWithoutEffect": "A parameter marked acceptedWithoutEffect is declared but read by no implementation. It is declared so the gap is visible instead of hiding behind a tolerant reader — NOT because it works, and each one is recorded as a finding. Resolve it by giving the parameter an effect or by retiring it in a new fixture; do not quietly bend an existing one. As of 2026-08-16 exactly one remains: balanceSheet.incomeMapping, and there by decision — see the comment on it."
  },
  "$operationsComment": "The same contract for the operations (F-9, reported by an embedding app). `PROJECTION_PARAMETERS` had declared every projection parameter since 0.7 while `execute()` read what it recognised and ignored the rest — so a typo in a projection parameter was an immediate E_INPUT_INVALID naming the parameter, and a typo in an operation input was silence plus a default. That asymmetry is the wrong way round: a projection returns a wrong number, an operation writes one into the books. Three defects came out of it before the contract existed — `expandTax` taking `date` where its neighbours take `voucherDate`, a wrong `direction` booking an invoice inverted (IMPL-013), and every numeric input read with a typeof check, so a form's \"30\" was not rejected but ignored while the default stood. `actor` is declared on every operation that writes an audit record, because that is what reads it.",
